e many standard deviations  from the mean and just probabilistically  the larger population you have putting  into the putting into a sport the the  more standard deviations you should  expect to see in terms of performance so  you know if you just start with the  assumption that the average person after  years of good training may be capable of  a 300 blokes score I'm not necessarily  saying that's what I think I'm just  saying we'll start with that assumption  because it makes the numbers much easier  and let's say that the average person or  that the standard deviation in terms of  performance is about 50 whelps points so  about two thirds of people would fall  somewhere between 250 and 350 and for  people they don't know Wilkes Score is  the way that performance is normalized  taking body weight into account for  powerlifting so roughly you know you so  you can compare people in higher weight  classes and lower weight classes so okay  let's start with that assumption with a  pretty small population you know you may  expect to see plenty of people one  standard deviation from the mean maybe a  person or two two standard deviations  from the mean with a 400 Wilkes that  would be about two and a half percent of  the population within two standard  deviations about ninety-five percent of  people would fall so you would expect  about half of that five percent to be  out of 400 books or above so you know if  you have a population of a thousand  lifters in a sport you should expect to  find quite a few people two standard  deviations from the mean but probably  you would be very very unlikely to find  someone four standard deviations from  the mean with a 500 book score or some  of the best in the world are now  cracking as 600 books who are at least  getting very very close to it with a  population of a thousand lifters it  would be incredibly unlikely  to find someone that good then if you  make the population ten times bigger a  hundred times bigger regardless of how  much of an outlier you're talking about  it becomes much much more likely you're  going to stumble across one of those  people now you know if if you just  picked random people out of a population  and said like you know how is there  going to be a freak power lifter here it  very well could be that the first person  you picked just randomly was Edie cone  or Jessi Norris that's entirely possible  but it's very very unlikely and just  simply the larger population you're  working with the more likely it is that  you're going to find those freaks in  that population and now comparing  powerlifting to other sports why do you  see records fall even so often and so  quickly in powerlifting compared to  other sports a couple things there one  there are just more weight classes and  divisions I mean you know in terms of  like 100-meter sprint there is the  hundred meter sprint you have a male and  female division and you have I think  like some masters and submasters  divisions but there's there's one event  in powerlifting you have a ton of weight  classes you have you know submasters  1-2-3 Junior teen masters police fire  military like there are a ton of  divisions for records to be set you also  have different standards of competition  single ply for all role with knee wraps  so that gives you many more  opportunities for records to be set but  then also even if we're gonna mix all of  that and just talk about say IPF World  Records or all-time World Records or  anything like that you're still dealing  you're still dealing with more classes  but also you're dealing with a younger  sport and going back to the original  point you're dealing with a sport that's  currently experiencing an explosion in  exposure and athlete pool so you know  there's there's probably not that large  of a difference in terms of people  competing in marathons today versus ten  years ago but they're probably well  there  definitely is for powerlifting there are  many more people powerlifting